Sweep every gate for a missing pre-flight: three infrastructure conditions in one batch were reported as code defects, and the fix is never in the checker that fired


## Three instances in one batch, 2026-09-01 — a class, not a coincidence

| incident | what the tool said | what was actually true |
|---|---|---|
| B106 | "your code does not compile" | x-alpha's disk was full |
| the name-filtered run | a green `test result:` summary | `--test <name>` filtered every test out |
| B132 | "encountered diff marker" | a peer session was mid-merge in the shared checkout |

**The shape is always the same: the tool reports what it saw rather than why it
could not see.** And in every case **the fix was not in the checker that fired** —
it was a missing PRE-FLIGHT. The checker was working correctly on the input it
received; nothing had established that the input was worth checking.

Two of the three were caught only by a number that failed to reconcile, which is
luck dressed as process.

## Deliverable

**One sweep of every gate and probe entry point**, asking of each: *what
condition could make this measure nothing, or measure the wrong thing, while
still returning a verdict?* Then add the pre-flight that refuses by name.

Candidates to examine, at minimum:
- `scripts/test-suite.py` — has the filtered-out guard (added after incident 2)
  and the binaries-reported reconciliation. Does it cover a zero-binary run, a
  missing typedb fixture, a `HOME` that is not the isolated one?
- `scripts/clippy_delta.py` — has the zero-workspace-units guard (which is what
  caught incident 3). Does it verify the baseline's toolchain matches the current
  one, rather than only recording it?
- `scripts/remote-build.py` — B132 added the git-operation pre-flight and B106
  added the free-space floor. What else silently degrades: a stale remote tree, a
  half-finished rsync, a remote `HOME` that moved?
- The probe entry points — `issue_repro_game`, `probe_enum_corpus`,
  `probe_export_binding`, `probe_shared_modifiers`, `probe_const_receiver`,
  `probe_member_visibility`, `probe_engine_const_receiver`. B118/B123/B127
  hardened three of these; the rest have not been examined for this specifically.

## The rule to apply, stated once

**An empty result is not a clean result.** Every gate must be able to
distinguish "I looked and found nothing wrong" from "I could not look". Where it
cannot, that is the finding.

## Do not fix by adding a warning

A warning that fires on a common, legitimate condition becomes one nobody reads —
B132 made this call correctly, printing `dirty=N` in the banner rather than
warning on it, because a dirty tree is the normal lane workflow. **Refuse on the
condition that makes the measurement meaningless; report everything else as
data.**

## Test axes

Each pre-flight needs its pair, and needs mutation:
- The bad condition -> refuses, and NAMES the condition.
- The legitimate look-alike -> proceeds. This is the row that stops the cure
  being worse than the disease, and B132's `dirty=4` gate run is the model.
- **Mutation:** delete the pre-flight and confirm the bad condition reaches the
  gate. Include an over-condemn mutation — a pre-flight that refuses everything
  passes a suite that only checks things get refused.

This batch found guards that could not fire in four separate tickets. Assume any
new pre-flight is vacuous until a mutation run says otherwise.